WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.043 --> 00:00:03.660
- So what was the Gilded
Age and why did it happen?

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.070
- Ah, the Gilded Age is
this fascinating period

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.640
from about 1870 to 1900,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.000
you can change the dates
a little bit but that's,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.600
so we're talking post Civil War,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.430
America becomes an industrial
powerhouse, the cities rise.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.880
So in 1850 fewer people live in the cities

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.404
than in the rural part of the country,

00:00:22.404 --> 00:00:26.000
by 1900 more people live in the city.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.320
And basically you have the
birth of the railroads,

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.380
the railroads get connected in 1869,

00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:34.160
continental railroad going all
the way across the country.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.320
You have the rise of
oil and John Rockefeller

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.520
and basically, this period,

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.640
think of Rockefeller and Vanderbilt, as,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.720
and Carnegie and JP Morgan as powerful

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:47.840
the way we think of presidents.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.170
Presidents during this period
kind of were on the descent

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.780
but the magnets of industry,
railroad, oil, steel,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.220
those were all banking,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.650
those were the superheroes in America,

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:02.237
and they led to this amazing
growth in industrialization

00:01:02.237 --> 00:01:05.135
but then also huge disparities.

00:01:05.135 --> 00:01:09.280
No labor laws that we would
be familiar with today.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.960
So you had this
industrialization with people

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.920
working in the industries
who had no protections,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.490
child labor, working 22 hours a day,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.263
horrible health conditions.

00:01:19.263 --> 00:01:23.328
So this gurgling, booming America,

00:01:23.328 --> 00:01:25.680
but where there's great disparities

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.740
between who's doing well and who's not.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.200
- And it was called Gilded
because those who were doing well

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.000
were living very well.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.140
- Right, Gilded as in a gilded
frame, covered with cold

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.350
which came from a novel by Mark Twain.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.500
And that's right, it takes on

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.793
the caste of this extraordinary wealth.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.920
Rockefeller was the first
billionaire in America,

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.710
Vanderbilt built the biggest house,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.460
still the biggest house in
America during this period.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.680
So the wealth was, people would
make eight to $10 in a week,

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.890
some of these tycoons were
making eight to $10 in a minute.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.660
And so that kind of vast wealth,

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.510
because you could only
make so much wealth before,

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.930
this is just mountains of wealth

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.290
and then also, again,
this great disparity.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.480
- And it seems like it came
from, really, technology.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.580
Technology allowed all
of this productivity,

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.480
the railroads, steel,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.270
and then of course finance
was able to get in there

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.680
and help move capital more efficiently.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.610
What parallels do you
see with our current age

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.800
where technology seems to
be doing something similar

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.380
where we have all of these new
industries, and new wealth,

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.800
but some fear that it might
be causing some inequality.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.810
- You have, yes, you
have a couple of things.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.990
You have innovation in these
very different industries,

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.480
both innovation in the creation of things

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.340
but then also innovations in
the structures of business.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.220
Buying up small business,
creating big conglomerates.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.582
Then using that leverage and power

00:02:52.582 --> 00:02:55.760
to then crowd out competitors for sure

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.600
but then also raise prices

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.729
because you're the only game in town.

00:02:59.729 --> 00:03:03.580
And you also have business
practices that are not

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.340
the sort of laissez-faire
economic belief said essentially

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.110
that in the economy it was
like in the American system,

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:13.990
which was let it operate.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:15.640
Don't get in the way, don't mess with it,

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:16.810
because when it operates

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.300
it runs the most efficiently for America.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:20.820
- In the end markets can be messy

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.260
but they're gonna have the best outcome.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.093
- That's right.

00:03:23.093 --> 00:03:24.330
- Is the argument behind laissez-faire.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.930
- That's exactly right, and
it got this wonderful assist

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.360
from Charles Darwin who said,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.140
we can explain the growth of,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.930
or you can explain the species

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:34.840
and we can explain our natural world

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.550
with this theory about the
competition among the species

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.880
and this term survival of the
fittest, social Darwinism,

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.470
survival of the fittest, which some,

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.870
and I certainly thought might've
come from Darwin didn't,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.280
it came from Herbert Spencer

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:50.220
who basically had an economic theory

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.510
of the survival of the fittest
and then it went this way.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:54.190
Some people have more talent than others

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.780
and when they exercise their
talent they do very well

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.920
and that's the best thing for society.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.390
It believed that society
was ever increasing,

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:03.900
it didn't mean that every
single person was increasing,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.830
but that if you followed
survival of the fittest

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.410
and the best people did the best

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.365
then ultimately society will always be

00:04:09.365 --> 00:04:11.619
on an evolutionary plane of moving upward.

00:04:11.619 --> 00:04:14.440
And so that was the theory behind

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:18.430
get out of the way of these
big companies and big tycoons

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.900
and they will do the best for America

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:23.260
and so the reason that was important

00:04:24.607 --> 00:04:26.060
was A, it kept government out of the way,

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:31.060
B, it kept religious,
it created a religion,

00:04:31.245 --> 00:04:33.900
it was a secular religion of course,

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.490
but it created a theory that said,

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.150
wow that looks like what you're doing

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.280
is totally self interested but
there's this theory behind it

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.030
and everybody will
improve so okay, go ahead.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.630
- Yeah, that was the beginning

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.850
of Gordon Gekko's famous greed is good.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.330
- Right, right, yeah.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:53.860
Yeah, greed is good, so the
comparison to our current moment

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:58.860
is you have huge disparity
and technologies that are,

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.830
to use a cliche of the day, disruptive

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.350
that are completely changing

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.010
the way everybody does business,

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.980
changing when we think about the way

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.983
in which Americans behave,
that rapid sense of change,

00:05:11.983 --> 00:05:14.110
it's changing culture rapidly

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:16.550
and it's making big winners and losers

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:19.440
and those big disparities exist as well.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.950
- How did the Gilded Age play out?

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.920
Were some of these forces
moderated eventually,

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:26.370
maybe around 1900,

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:29.750
and do you think similar
things might happen for us?

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:31.960
- There were two big moderation

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.010
in response to the Gilded Age.

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:37.200
You had government came awake again

00:05:38.587 --> 00:05:40.815
and then you also had labor movements

00:05:40.815 --> 00:05:45.230
that came into formation,

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:47.877
basically to slow down the growth

00:05:47.877 --> 00:05:50.430
and the rapacious demands
of the Gilded Age.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.970
Politics during the
Gilded Age kind of went,

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:57.330
became an offshoot of
the titans of industry.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:02.040
If you look at the presidency
between 1876 and 1892

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.860
they're all one term presidents

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.670
and none of them get
more than 50% of the vote

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.400
and basically what the
presidents spend their time doing

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.550
is using the spoil system,

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.328
which is essentially
putting people in jobs

00:06:13.328 --> 00:06:16.840
to pay off the local bosses
who helped them get elected.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:20.870
So getting elected became
a job of staying elected

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.862
and that meant dolling out patronage,

00:06:22.862 --> 00:06:26.010
basically giving people
jobs who were your friends

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:27.380
so that they would go and vote for you

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:29.870
because these elections
were all very close

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.930
and that's not getting a lot
of work done for the people.

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.010
Some of 'em, Rutherford B. Hayes

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.760
tried these little efforts
at civil service reform,

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.110
which essentially meant
putting people in jobs

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.120
who could monitor the factories,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.100
make sure that people
weren't getting abused

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:47.700
or that health wasn't declining

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.240
or that anything that
a government might do

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:51.070
that we think of today

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.212
but his political
patrons didn't want that.

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:55.590
So that was all very hard to do.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:58.542
What happened on the worker's
end is they realized,

00:06:58.542 --> 00:07:02.570
and the most famous moment was in 1911,

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:04.690
the Triangle Shirtwaist Fire where

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.127
a number of women working
in a garment factory are,

00:07:07.127 --> 00:07:12.127
there's a fire and the
bosses lock the doors

00:07:12.861 --> 00:07:17.830
and I think 40 some odd
women die, mostly women,

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:21.720
and it highlighted the labor issues

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.460
but labor unions start to organize

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.940
and there are huge clashes and
strikes and consumer boycotts

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.468
and those start to put
some pressure on business

00:07:29.468 --> 00:07:34.040
to change their practices, at
least in terms of worker hours

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:35.730
and the kinds of things that we now

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:37.770
would certainly take for granted.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:39.640
- Fascinating.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.490
- It's an incredible period.
- Yeah.

